    title: { zh: "六本 15.2", en: "Li Wang's Temple and Two Mourners" },
    text: {
      zh: `孔子曰：「此必厘王之廟。」公曰：「何以知之？」孔子曰：「《詩》云：『皇皇上天，其命不忒。天之以善，必報其德。』禍亦如之。夫厘王變文武之制，而作玄黃華麗之飾；宮室崇峻，輿馬奢侈；而弗可振也。故天殃所宜加其廟焉，以是占之為然。」公曰：「天何不殃其身，而加罰其廟也？」孔子曰：「蓋以文、武故也。若殃其身，則文武之嗣，無乃殄平。故當殃其廟，以彰其過。」俄頃，左右報曰：「所災者，厘王廟也。」景公驚起，再拜曰：「善哉！聖人之智，過人遠矣。」子夏三年之喪畢，見於孔子。子曰：「與之琴，使之弦。」侃侃而樂，作而曰：「先王制禮，不敢不及。」子曰：「君子也。」閔子三年之喪畢，見於孔子。子曰：「與之琴，使之弦。」切切而悲，作而曰：「先王制禮，弗敢過也。」子曰：「君子也。」子貢曰：「閔子哀未盡，夫子曰：『君子也。』子夏哀已盡，又曰：『君子也。』二者殊情而俱曰『君子』。賜也惑，敢問之。」孔子曰：「閔子哀未忘，能斷之以禮。子夏哀已盡，能引之及禮。`,
      en: `Confucius said, "This must be the temple of King Li."

The duke said, "How do you know?"

Confucius said, "The Odes say: 'Bright bright Heaven — its mandate is not errant. Heaven's favor to the good must repay their virtue' — calamity likewise. King Li altered Wen and Wu's institutions, making dark-yellow gorgeous adornments; palaces lofty, chariots and horses extravagant — yet he could not be restored. Therefore Heaven's disaster was fittingly placed on his temple; judged thus, it is so."

The duke said, "Why does Heaven not strike his person but punish his temple?"

Confucius said, "It is because of Wen and Wu. If it struck his person, Wen and Wu's line might be extinguished. Therefore the temple was struck to display his fault."

Shortly after, attendants reported, "What burned was King Li's temple." Duke Jing started up and bowed twice, saying, "Excellent! The sage's wisdom surpasses others by far."

When Zixia's three-year mourning was complete and he visited Confucius, the Master gave him a zither and had him play. He played with ease and joy, then rose and said, "The former kings fixed ritual — I dare not fall short." The Master said, "A gentleman."

When Minzi's three-year mourning was complete and he visited Confucius, the Master gave him a zither and had him play. He played with keen sorrow, then rose and said, "The former kings fixed ritual — I dare not exceed it." The Master said, "A gentleman."

Zigong said, "Minzi's grief is not yet exhausted, yet you call him a gentleman; Zixia's grief is already exhausted, yet you also call him a gentleman. Their feelings differ, yet both are called 'gentleman.' I am confused and dare ask."

Confucius said, "Minzi's grief is not forgotten — he can cut it off by ritual. Zixia's grief is already exhausted — he can draw it back to ritual. Though equally gentlemen, is it not acceptable?"`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "孔子說：這一定是釐王的廟。景公問何以知之。孔子說：《詩》云「皇皇上天，其命不忒；天之以善，必報其德」——禍也是如此。釐王改變文王、武王的制度，製作玄黃華麗的裝飾，宮室高大、車馬奢侈，卻不可挽救；所以天殃應當加在他的廟上，以此占斷便是如此。景公問：天何不殃及其身，卻罰他的廟呢？孔子說：大概是因為文、武的緣故。若殃及其身，文武王的後嗣豈不要滅絕？所以應當殃及其廟，以彰顯他的過失。過了一會兒，左右稟報：受災的正是釐王廟。景公驚起，再拜說：善哉！聖人的智慧遠超人了。子夏三年之喪完畢，來見孔子。孔子給他琴，讓他彈。他侃侃而樂，起身說：先王制禮，不敢不及。孔子說：君子也。閔子三年之喪完畢，來見孔子。孔子給他琴，讓他彈。他切切而悲，起身說：先王制禮，不敢超過。孔子也說：君子也。子貢疑惑：閔子哀未盡，夫子稱君子；子夏哀已盡，又稱君子——二者感情不同卻同稱君子，敢問何故。孔子說：閔子哀尚未忘，卻能用禮來裁斷；子夏哀已經盡，卻能把它引回到禮。孔子以釐王變亂文武之制、奢侈不可振，斷天殃當加其廟；又解釋不殃其身是為保全文武王之嗣，俄頃驗證，景公再拜稱聖智。後半轉入喪後鼓琴：子夏哀盡而樂，自謂不敢不及禮；閔子哀未盡而悲，自謂不敢過禮——孔子皆稱君子。子貢惑於「殊情同稱」，孔子對以「能斷之以禮」與「能引之及禮」，章末句子未完，下節以「雖均之君子」收束。",
      en: "Confucius said: this must be King Li's temple. Asked how he knew, he cited the Ode: bright Heaven's mandate does not err; Heaven's favor to the good must repay their virtue — and calamity likewise. King Li altered Wen and Wu's institutions, made dark-yellow gorgeous adornments, lofty palaces, extravagant chariots and horses, yet could not be restored; therefore Heaven's disaster was fittingly placed on his temple — judged thus, it is so. Asked why Heaven did not strike his person but punish his temple, Confucius said it was because of Wen and Wu: if it struck his person, Wen and Wu's line might be extinguished; therefore the temple was struck to display his fault. Shortly after, attendants reported what burned was indeed King Li's temple. Duke Jing started up, bowed twice, and said: excellent — the sage's wisdom far surpasses others. When Zixia's three-year mourning was complete and he visited Confucius, the Master gave him a zither to play. He played with ease and joy, then rose and said: the former kings fixed ritual — I dare not fall short. The Master said: a gentleman. When Minzi's three-year mourning was complete and he visited, the Master likewise had him play. He played with keen sorrow, then rose and said: the former kings fixed ritual — I dare not exceed it. The Master again said: a gentleman. Zigong was confused: Minzi's grief is not yet exhausted, yet you call him a gentleman; Zixia's grief is already exhausted, yet you also call him a gentleman — different feelings, same name — he dared ask why. Confucius said: Minzi's grief is not forgotten — he can cut it off by ritual; Zixia's grief is already exhausted — he can draw it back to ritual. Confucius judges from King Li altering Wen and Wu's institutions and irreparable luxury that Heaven's disaster should fall on his temple; not striking his person preserves Wen and Wu's line — soon verified, and Duke Jing bows twice to sage wisdom. The latter half turns to zither after mourning: Zixia's grief exhausted, playing with ease, daring not to fall short of ritual; Minzi's grief not exhausted, playing with sorrow, daring not to exceed ritual — both called gentlemen. Zigong puzzled by different feelings under one name hears \"cut off by ritual\" and \"draw back to ritual\"; the unit's last sentence is unfinished, closed next by \"though equally gentlemen.\""
    },
    application: {
      zh: "同稱「君子」不必情狀相同；關鍵在能否以禮節情——哀盡而能引之及禮，未盡而能斷之以禮，皆可。\n<br /><b>建議：若你正處喪失或結束儀式，問自己：是需要「收斂以合禮」，還是「延續以盡情而不逾節」？</b>",
      en: "Both may be called gentlemen without identical feeling; the point is ritual's measure of grief — drawing exhausted sorrow back to rite, or cutting lingering sorrow by rite.\n<br /><b>Suggestion: In loss or after a rite, ask whether you need to gather feeling to fit ritual, or extend it without overstepping.</b>"
    }
